<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Ensemble Models</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By comparing the performance of watermarking models trained with CNN and DCT-Transformer, as shown in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.2. In-Processing Watermarking ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ Enhancing Robustness in Post-Processing Watermarking: An Ensemble Attack Network Using CNNs and Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we observe that each watermarking model excels under different types of distortions. To enhance the robustness of the watermarking model against diverse distortions, we explore the combination of CNN and DCT-Transformer in four configurations: <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Model Cascade, Model Parallel, Aggregate Blend</span>, and <span id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Random Blend</span>. The detailed procedures of these four ensemble methods are illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3. Analysis of Spatial and Frequency Domains ‣ 3. The Proposed Method ‣ Enhancing Robustness in Post-Processing Watermarking: An Ensemble Attack Network Using CNNs and Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Cascade.</span> During each iteration of the training process, the watermarked image <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is initially passed through the DCT-Transformer, and then forwarded to CNN for distortion generation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model Parallel.</span> In this dual-path setup, the watermarked image <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is randomly passed through either the DCT-Transformer or the CNN based on a predetermined threshold <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each iteration, a random value in the interval [0,1] is generated. If the value exceeds the threshold <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, the watermarked image <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> will be processed by the DCT-Transformer; otherwise, it is passed through the CNN.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Random Blend.</span> To preserve the original pixel information, both the cover image <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{co}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{co}</annotation></semantics></math> and the watermarked image <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> are used as inputs to the attack network. For each image, a random threshold <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{co}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{co}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{co}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{co}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to decide whether it will be processed by the DCT-Transformer or the CNN. Importantly, in each iteration, exactly one of the two images is passed through the DCT-Transformer and the other through the CNN, ensuring a complementary processing path. After generating <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{adv}^{co}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{adv}^{co}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{adv}^{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mi>w</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{adv}^{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, the two outputs are blended using a blending factor <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> according to the equation <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{adv}=\beta\cdot I_{adv}^{w}+(1-\beta)\cdot I_{adv}^{co}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mi>w</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{adv}=\beta\cdot I_{adv}^{w}+(1-\beta)\cdot I_{adv}^{co}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is randomly generated in each iteration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aggregate Blend.</span> In contrast to the Random Blend method, both the cover image <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{co}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{co}</annotation></semantics></math> and the watermarked image <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> are passed through the DCT-Transformer and the CNN, resulting in two sets of adversarial images, <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{adv}^{co}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{adv}^{co}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{adv}^{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mi>w</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{adv}^{w}</annotation></semantics></math>, each containing two adversarial images. We randomly select one image from each sets and blend the selected images together using a blending coefficient <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, the final adversarial output <math id="S3.SS4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{adv}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{adv}</annotation></semantics></math> is an aggregation of many possible combinations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Among four ensemble strategies, Model Parallel (MP) attains the highest average bit accuracy, as shown in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.3. Analysis of Spatial and Frequency Domains ‣ 3. The Proposed Method ‣ Enhancing Robustness in Post-Processing Watermarking: An Ensemble Attack Network Using CNNs and Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, suggesting that balanced exposure to diverse distortions helps prevent overfitting to any single distortion characteristic and thereby improves overall robustness. This hypothesis is further supported by the observation that the performance of the watermarking model trained with Model Parallel (MP) generally lies between those trained exclusively with CNN or DCT-Transformer attack networks. For instance, it outperforms the CNN in handling compression distortions while remaining comparable to the DCT-Transformer. These findings are further validated by comparisons under various distortion levels, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.4. Ensemble Models ‣ 3. The Proposed Method ‣ Enhancing Robustness in Post-Processing Watermarking: An Ensemble Attack Network Using CNNs and Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
